<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elijah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elijah was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> prophet of the 9th century BCE in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, known from the biblical Books of Kings. He opposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a> and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezebel">Jezebel</a> and their promotion of the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>, most famously in the contest on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Mount Carmel</a>, and was said to have been taken up to heaven in a whirlwind. He is honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elijah.jpg" alt="Elijah" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Girolamo Savoldo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Girolamo_Savoldo,_Elijah_Fed_by_the_Raven,_c._1510,_NGA_46134.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prophets formed an independent voice that could challenge kings: <b>Elijah</b> opposed Ahab and Jezebel’s promotion of Baal, and Hosea condemned the court’s shifting alliances.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Canaan, 'great' from its commercial relations, famous for its manufactures and arts, and from the time of David waning as Tyre, its 'virgin daughter', rose; <b>Elijah</b> sojourned there, Jesus visited the coasts of Tyre and Sidon, and Paul's ship put in at Sidon on the way to Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Elijah</b> of Vilna (1720–1789) warned singers not to pause in the middle of one phrase, since a pause changed its sense.</blockquote><a href="/songs/shalom-aleichem">Shalom Aleichem</a></li><li><blockquote>Willis may have been inspired by the sight of the Red River, beside which he was labouring, which reminded him of the Jordan and of the prophet <b>Elijah</b> taken up to heaven in a chariot (2 Kings 2:11).</blockquote><a href="/songs/swing-low-sweet-chariot">Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</a></li><li><blockquote>It took its name from <b>Elijah</b> Shaw in 1870.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The abolitionist editor <b>Elijah</b> Lovejoy was shot defending his fourth printing press from a mob in Alton, Illinois.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
